#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/home-outside-home.md:0
msgid ""
"Understand snapd support for home directories outside /home, using the "
"homedirs system option or bind mounts for snap user data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/home-outside-home.md:8
msgid "Home outside home"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/home-outside-home.md:10
msgid ""
"The snap daemon (snapd) looks for a user's home directory ($HOME) under `/"
"home` on the local filesystem. However, from _snapd 2.59_ onwards, the snap "
"daemon can access an additional location using the `homedirs` {ref}`system "
"option <how-to-guides-manage-snaps-set-system-options>`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/home-outside-home.md:12
msgid "sudo snap set system homedirs=<destination-directory>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/home-outside-home.md:15
msgid ""
"This allows a snap's user data to be stored in a user's home location other "
"under `/home`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/home-outside-home.md:17
msgid ""
"The following command will permit home directories to be accessible from `/"
"remote/users`, for instance:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/home-outside-home.md:19
msgid "sudo snap set system homedirs=/remote/users\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/home-outside-home.md:22
msgid ""
"The new location needs to exist and be accessible, but it can be on a "
"different filesystem or even mounted across a network. The original `/home` "
"location remains valid but it is no longer a requirement that directories be "
"stored there."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/home-outside-home.md:24
msgid ""
"Once set, the `homedirs` system option can be retrieved with the `snap get` "
"command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/home-outside-home.md:26
msgid ""
"$ sudo snap get system homedirs\n"
"/remote/users\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/home-outside-home.md:31
msgid ""
"The `homedirs` value can be cleared and restored to only `/home` with the "
"`snap unset` command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/home-outside-home.md:33
msgid "sudo snap unset system homedirs\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/home-outside-home.md:37
msgid "Bind mount home directories"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/home-outside-home.md:39
msgid ""
"While the _homedirs_ system option should work for the majority users, it's "
"also possible to _bind mount_ an alternative $HOME location to `/home` to "
"allow other locations to be found by snapd. This process is outlined below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/home-outside-home.md:41
msgid ""
"ⓘ  A **bind mount** allows a mounted filesystem to be accessible from more "
"than one location at the filesystem level. This is unlike a hard or symbolic "
"link, for instance, which operate as special additional files that point to "
"a destination."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/home-outside-home.md:43
msgid ""
"There are two steps to bind mount a home directory to a different location:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/home-outside-home.md:44
msgid "**the bind mount:** create the mount point and run the _mount_ command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/home-outside-home.md:45
msgid ""
"$ sudo mkdir -p /home/$USER\n"
"$ sudo mount --bind <original-home-location> /home/$USER\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/home-outside-home.md:49
msgid ""
"**edit `/etc/passwd`**: backup `passwd` and edit the home location for your "
"user:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/home-outside-home.md:50
msgid ""
"$ cp /etc/passwd passwd.backup\n"
"# sudo edit /etc/passwd with your favourite editor\n"
"$ cat /etc/passwd | grep $USER\n"
"  ubuntu:x:1000:1000:ubuntu,,,:/home/ubuntu:/bin/bash\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/home-outside-home.md:56
msgid ""
"The following `awk` command can be used to edit `/etc/passwd` (change "
"OLD_HOME to your old home directory):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/home-outside-home.md:57
msgid ""
"$ awk -vold=$\"OLD_HOME\" -vnew=$\"/home/$USER\" -F: ' BEGIN {OFS = \":\"} "
"\\\n"
"  {sub(old,new,$6);print}' /etc/passwd > passwd.new\n"
"$ sudo cp passwd.new /etc/passwd\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/home-outside-home.md:63
msgid ""
"Log out and back in again, and snap will work from the freshly mounted home "
"location. If you run into difficulties, copy the backup passwd file to `/etc/"
"passwd`."
msgstr ""
